==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|insetten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|insettan||to set in, institute, appoint}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|in|set}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|inzetten||to insert, set in}}, {{cog|nds|insetten||to set in}}, {{cog|de|einsetzen||to insert, employ}}, {{cog|da|indsætte||to insert}}, {{cog|sv|insätta||to inset, induct, institute}}, {{cog|is|innsetja||to install}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪnsɛt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-inset.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-inset.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪnsɛt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|insets|insetting|inset|past2=insetted}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[set]] in; [[infix]] or [[implant]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[insert]] something.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To add an inset to something. <!--what is the object of this transitive sense?-->

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A smaller thing set into a larger thing, such as a small [[picture]] inside a larger one.
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=The '''inset''' of figure 1 shows the geometry of the samples.|title=Observation and an Explanation of Breakdown of the Quantum Hall Effect|work=Journal of Research of the National Institute of Standards and Technology|year=1990|author=M. E. Cage|issue=1|volume=95|author2=D. Y. Yu|author3=G. Marullo Reedtz}}
# Anything inserted.
# A small piece of [[material]] used to [[strengthen]] a [[garment]].
# {{lb|en|telecommunication}} A modular microphone that can be removed from a telephone handset without disassembly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Andrew Emmerson|title=Electronic Classics: Collecting, Restoring and Repair|page=99
|passage=Microphone '''insets''' can deteriorate and older examples may produce a permanent frying noise.}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} An opening partway down a [[shaft]], giving access to the intermediate levels.

